





               These release notes  pertain  to  both  the  software  and  the
          documentation  set  for the Digital Research product, PL/I-86.  They
          provide the most current information regarding this product  in  two
          areas:

             o Sofware bugs that have been identified since  the  product  was
               released.

             o Errors or omissions in the documentation set that could not  be
               corrected  because  of the lead time needed for typesetting and
               printing.














































                                          1







                                       SOFTWARE 


          The following features are described in the documentaton  set  which
          applies  to  both  PL/I-80  V1.4  and  PL/I-86  V1.0,  but  are  NOT
          implemented in PL/I-86 V1.0:

             o Double-precision floating-point arithmetic **

             o File password protection in the ENVIRONMENT option

             o File and record locking using  the  built-in  LOCK  and  UNLOCK
               functions.


               **  NOTE!!! NOTE!!! NOTE!!! NOTE!!! NOTE!!! NOTE!!! NOTE!!!

               PL/I-86 uses the IEEE format  for  representing  floating-point
               numbers.  This  means  there  is  a fundamental incompatibility
               between PL/I-80 and PL/I-86.  A  PL/I-86  program  cannot  read
               floating-point  DATA  written  to disk files with PL/I-80.  See
               the  section  below  concerning  the   documentation   in   the
               Programmer's Guide.

                    To convert from PL/I-80 single precision  floating  point
               format  to  PL/I-86 single precision floating point format, you
               can use the following procedure:


               SP80TO86: procedure(f) returns(float binary(24));
               declare (f,r) float binary(24);
               declare (fp,rp) pointer;
               declare 1 f80 based(fp), 2 (word0,word1) bit(16);
               declare 1 f86 based(rp), 2 (word0,word1) bit(16);
               declare 1 f80over based(fp), 2 (byte0,byte1,byte2,byte3) fixed bin(7);
               declare b fixed bin(7);

               fp = addr(f);
               rp = addr(r);

               /* copy the whole source to target */
               r = f;

               /* copy exponent and adjust bias by 2: 1 for 127 vs 128, 1 for 1. not .1 */
               b = byte3 - 2;
               substr(rp->f86.word1,2,8) = unspec(b);

               /* copy sign bit */
               substr(rp->f86.word1,1,1) = substr(fp->f80.word1,9,1);

               return(r);

               end;





                                          2







          The following are known software bugs in version 1.0:

             o A(constant-expression) = constant; gives a compile-time  error.
               You can program around this using the following mechanism:

                  i = constant-expression;
                  A(i) = constant;


             o A CALL statement cannot contain  a  null  argument  list.   For
               example,

                  CALL A();

               You can program around this by always using

                  CALL A;


             o There is an  incorrect  string  assignment  if  the  right-hand
               string  overlaps  the  left-hand  string.  The  example  in the
               Language Reference Manual, page 71 is correct; the Compiler  is
               not.

             o The  RANK  built-in  function  doesn't  accept  SUBSTR  as   an
               argument.  For  example,  RANK(SUBSTR(C,1,1))  gives a compile-
               time error message.

             o A statement in column 1 following a simple DO statement  causes
               a syntax error.

             o End-of-file is not noticed if  the  column  descriptor  in  the
               format specifies a location past the actual end of the file.

























                                          3







                                  DOCUMENTATION ERRATA



               In the PL/I Language Reference Manual and the PL/I Programmer's
          Guide, character and bit strings are denoted as:

                 'string`  instead of 'string'

               This is a  peculiarity  of  the  typeface  used  to  print  the
               manuals.  You  should  never  use the reverse apostrophe (`) to
               delimit a string.   Always  delimit  strings  with  the  single
               apostrophe character (').



                               PL/I Language Reference Manual


             o Section 3.1.2, page 29, the last paragraph states

                 Also, PL/I  signals  a  FIXED  OVERFLOW  error  if  a  value
                 assigned  to the variable has more significant digits to the
                 left of the decimal point than the declared precision of the
                 variable allows.

               Change this to read

                 Also, PL/I signals a  FIXEDOVERFLOW  condition  if  a  value
                 assigned  to the variable has more significant digits to the
                 left of the decimal point than the declared precision of the
                 variable allows.


             o Section 3.1.3, page 30, Table 3-1 shows the range of a  single-
               precision floating-point number as

                 5.88*10**-39 <= |x| <= 3.40*10**38

               This is correct for PL/I-80.  However, PL/I-86  uses  the  IEEE
               floating-point format, so the range should be

                 1.18*10**-38 <= |x| <= 3.40*10**38


             o Section 3.6, page 37, the second paragraph states

                 The  general  form  of  the  DECLARE  statement  for  scalar
                 variables is:

               Change this to read

                 The  simple  form  of  the  DECLARE  statement  for   scalar
                 variables is:




