                                   PL/I Programmer's Guide


             o Section 6, page 53, in Figure 6-1 change

                 &INCLUDE

               to

                 %INCLUDE


             o Section 17.2, page 240, Figure  17-2  is  correct  for  PL/I-80
               except 32 should be 31 as shown below

                 -------------------------
                 | exponent |s| mantissa |
                 -------------------------
                 31         23 22        0

               However, PL/I-86 uses  the  IEEE  floating-point  format.   The
               fields  are  the  same size, but the sign bit appears first, so
               for PL/I-86, Figure 17-2 should appear as

                 -------------------------
                 |s| exponent | mantissa |
                 -------------------------
                 31 30      23 22        0


               Also, in the  IEEE  floating-point  format,  the  bias  is  127
               (decimal),  and  the  low-order  bit  of the exponent is in the
               high-order  byte  of  the  mantissa.   Therefore,  change   the
               hexadecimal values in the example on page 241 to

                 ---------------------
                 | 00 | 00 | C0 | 3F |
                 ---------------------



             o Section 17.4, page 244, states

                 where b represents a blank, and x  represents  an  undefined
                 character position.

               Change this sentence to read

                 where b represents a blank.

               Also, change xxxx in both of the examples to bbbb.


             o Section 17.5, page 245, Figure  17-4  shows  storage  for  bit-
               strings incorrectly.  Figure 17-4 should be as follows:



                                          9








                                            low byte  high byte

                     BIT(8)                 BIT(16)

                     1000 0000              0000 0000 1000 0000

                     BIT(8)                 BIT(16)

                     1010 0000              0000 0000 1010 0000

                     BIT(8)                 BIT(16)

                     N/A                    0011 0100 0001 0010


             o Section 18.2.2, page 254, the example of returning a  floating-
               point  number  is  correct  for PL/I-80.  PL/I-86 uses the IEEE
               floating-point standard, so change the  hexadecimal  values  in
               the example to read

                 ---------------------
                 | 00 | 00 | C0 | 3F |
                 ---------------------


































